Project: Automated AWS Data Pipeline Using Amazon S3, AWS Lambda & AWS Glue

1. Project Overview
Developed an automated data pipeline using AWS services to process e-commerce order data.
Used Amazon S3 to store input CSV files and processed output data.
Used AWS Lambda to trigger an AWS Glue ETL job when a new file is uploaded to S3.
Used AWS Glue Studio to build a visual ETL workflow for data cleaning and transformation.
Stored the processed data in Amazon S3 and used AWS Glue Data Catalog to manage dataset metadata.

2. AWS Services Used
Amazon S3: Stores input CSV files and processed output data.
AWS Lambda: Triggers the AWS Glue ETL job when a new file is uploaded to S3.
AWS Glue: Performs ETL operations, including schema mapping, duplicate removal, and SQL transformations.
AWS Glue Data Catalog: Stores metadata about datasets, including table schemas and data locations.
Amazon Athena: Can be used to query the processed data using SQL.

3. AWS Glue ETL Workflow — What I Implemented
Step 1: Amazon S3 Data Source
Configured Amazon S3 as the source of the ETL workflow.
Read e-commerce order data from CSV files stored in S3.
Passed the source data to the subsequent transformation steps.

Step 2: Change Schema
Used the Change Schema transformation in AWS Glue Studio.
Mapped source columns to the target schema.
Configured appropriate data types for columns.
Kept columns such as orderid, customer, and item as strings.
Configured quantity as bigint and price as double.

Step 3: Drop Duplicates
Used the Drop Duplicates transformation to remove duplicate records.
Helped improve data quality by eliminating duplicate data according to the configured columns.

Step 4: SQL Query
Used the SQL Query transformation to apply business rules to the data.
Applied a filter to select records where price > 1000, if this is the query currently configured in the job.
Passed the filtered results to the output destinations.

Step 5: Amazon S3 Output
Configured an Amazon S3 target to store the transformed data.
Used S3 as the destination for processed data.
Organized output data in the configured S3 location.

Step 6: AWS Glue Data Catalog
Configured an AWS Glue Data Catalog target.
Used the Data Catalog to manage dataset metadata, including the table schema and data location.
Prepared the dataset for discovery and querying by compatible analytics services.

4. AWS Lambda Automation
Created a Lambda function named glue_trigger_s3.
Used Python and the AWS SDK for Python (boto3).
Configured the function to start the AWS Glue job named Glue_ETL_project.
Designed the pipeline to respond automatically to new S3 object uploads through an S3 event notification.
Connected the storage and ETL services to reduce the need for manual job execution.

5. Data Flow
Upload an input CSV file to Amazon S3.
S3 sends an object creation event to AWS Lambda.
Lambda starts the AWS Glue ETL job.
Glue reads the source data.
Glue applies schema mapping and removes duplicates.
Glue runs the SQL transformation.
Glue writes the processed data to the configured S3 and Data Catalog destinations.

